  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Mechanistic Interpretability of Deep Neural Networks: Methods, Frameworks, and Perspectives",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13868",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.13868v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Deep neural networks underpin fundamental tasks in a wide range of signal domains, such as image, audio, and language processing. However, their internal mechanisms are notably difficult to interpret. Internal activations are structured signals transformed across layers that may encode a multitude of interdependent relationships, as individual neurons frequently represent multiple underlying concepts. Mechanistic interpretability aims to uncover the internal mechanisms of neural networks by identifying components whose activations are causally responsible for specific behaviors. In such a context, this article provides an overview of advances in mechanistic interpretability for the analysis of causal inference in deep neural networks.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-11-11T05:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Mechanistic Interpretability Needs a Logic of Inference",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14483",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.14483v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Neural networks are increasingly studied not merely as predictive systems, but as complex systems whose internal computations are themselves objects of scientific inquiry. Recent progress in mechanistic interpretability (MI) has made it possible to identify neurons, features, circuits, population structures, and dynamical patterns associated with model behavior. As these tools improve, attention is shifting from what can be observed inside a model to how those observations support mechanistic explanations. We examine the parallel efforts of neuroscience and MI to address this question and identify recurring inferential bottlenecks when inferring mechanisms from complex neural systems. We propose five principles and organize them into a recursive logic for a more rigorous procedure of mechanistic inference. These principles provide a framework that supports scientific discovery in two senses: it helps turn local interpretability findings into cumulative knowledge of mechanisms within models, and it makes those mechanisms sufficiently precise to generate testable hypotheses about domains beyond the models. Evidence beyond MI is ultimately required to establish what model mechanisms reveal about the world.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-11-11T05:00:00"
    }
  ]
}